  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nA near 600 dam upper high is almost stationary over the SE CONUS, \nbringing record heat for mid September. As we approach the weekend, \nthe upper high will scoot back west and eventually weaken. Surface \nhigh pressure over the E and NE, will also weaken, leaving our area \nmore susceptible to just enough instability for diurnal shower \ndevelopment. A slight outside chance of showers for the southern \nplateau and mountains of SW Virginia later today. Tomorrow may \nintroduce some scattered development over the Southern Appalachians \ninto SW Virginia. Precipitation chances for the afternoon and \nevening will persist into next week, possibly expanding in coverage.\n\nThe lower to mid (some upper) 90s are forecast through the weekend, \nbefore a slight turn downward towards the end of the forecast period \nor early next week. Upper 90s to low 100s heat indices will be \npossible from the southern valley up through the central valley. The \nhighest heat indices may peak Friday. Though most of the area won't \nreach Heat Advisory criteria, that doesn't mean to let your guard \ndown when it comes to to being outdoors, staying cool, and hydrated. \nOur daytime highs yesterday (before climate officially runs after \nthis discussion), tied daily records at both Tri-Cities and \nChattanooga. That makes Chattanooga's streak of 90 and above at 43 \ndays and counting, with no break in sight.",
